
# This is a constant
# Top level allows functions and constants only:

i8 CONSTANT = 4; // what to allow here? literal only to start

# fn keyword makes language not need backtracking
fn foo(i8 i, flt f) flt {  # Note return type at end
  if(i == 0) {
    return f;
  } else if (i == 1) {
    return 0.0;
  } else {
    panic "unexpected value of i";
  }
}

# Types:
flt  # double
## signed integers:
i8
i16
i32
## unsigned integers:
u8
u16
u32
## other:
str
obj  # c-sharp object
fn   # function handle.
## arrays:
<type>[] # where <type> is one of the above

# data structures
C-like if
C-like for
C-like while
C-like switch

# additional:
panic - unhandlable exception. essentially exit.

# Not in language:
# to keep it simple!
- generics
- closures, lambdas & anonymous functions
- exceptions

# interop
c-sharp objects can be made available as values with callable methods by annotating them and passing them to the VM.

# extensions
native structs

# notes
parent language is used for garbage collection


# Dev notes

Literal type inference
 - grammar only allows for statements where we already know the type
 - this means we can pass down the expected output type as we parse
 - boundaries are assignment, call arguments, flow control arguments...
 - this also means we need to know global and function signatures before we parse
   - either two passes required (initial just scrapes global signatures) OR need to forward declare.
   - two passes also means we can make global lookup a compile time thing (by index rather than by name).
